# Feature boundaries

Optimize for the smallest clear ownership boundary. Keep code close to the
route or feature that owns it; do not create abstractions to avoid a few
duplicated lines.

## Default layout

```text
app/(app)/feature/
  page.tsx              # Server Component and page-level data
  _components/          # UI used only by this feature
  _actions/             # Server actions and action-specific helpers
  _procedures/          # Feature-local oRPC procedures
modules/feature/         # Only for genuinely shared client state or behavior
```

## Placement decisions

- Fetch page-specific data in the Server Component and pass it to the UI.
- Keep interactive UI in a client component.
- Use an actionable oRPC procedure or a colocated Server Action for
  page-specific mutations.
- Use `modules/` only when the same state or behavior is used by multiple
  routes/components or must stay synchronized globally.
- Use React Query for shared client lifecycle, caching, polling, or invalidation.
  Keep its query options, hooks, and operations inside the owning module.
- Follow `orpc.mdc` for oRPC transport, caching, and authentication details.

## Simplification rules

- A one-page feature stays colocated, even if a shared-looking name is tempting.
- Duplicate a short query, auth check, or CRUD operation when that is clearer.
- Extract after the third real use, or when a complex 50+ line algorithm has a
  clear owner.
- Delete pass-through wrappers and speculative “service” or “operations”
  layers that add no decision or invariant.
- Preserve semantic HTML, framework boundaries, and existing public contracts
  while simplifying structure.
